00:04:00.229 --> 00:04:02.725
So I'll start with you, Graham.

00:04:02.725 --> 00:04:09.763
So coming from a background in mining,
why business school?

00:04:09.763 --> 00:04:12.251
&gt;&gt; It's good question.

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:17.301
I think after sort of working for
four years on operations as

00:04:17.301 --> 00:04:22.161
an engineer,
I said there are a couple pushing factors.

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:26.844
One was I wanted to understand
the business as a whole.

00:04:26.844 --> 00:04:28.988
I knew how operations worked.

00:04:28.988 --> 00:04:31.443
I knew how that side worked.

00:04:31.443 --> 00:04:34.260
I knew the planning of that and etc.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.559
I had no idea what happened on
the finance side of things.

00:04:36.559 --> 00:04:38.435
I had no idea what happened
on my supply chain event.

00:04:38.435 --> 00:04:42.685
I had no idea what happened in
human capital, human resources,

00:04:42.685 --> 00:04:45.058
how strategy decisions were made.

00:04:45.058 --> 00:04:47.020
All of these sort of things.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:52.436
And I wanted to get a better understanding
of that with a view that one day

00:04:52.436 --> 00:04:57.762
if I were to stay within mining and
sort of continue up the career path,

00:04:57.762 --> 00:05:01.641
my background might be in
an engineering side, but

00:05:01.641 --> 00:05:07.176
I would at least know and understand
what the rest of business was doing.

00:05:07.176 --> 00:05:12.234
And then secondly, the second main
reason was, the career path and

00:05:12.234 --> 00:05:17.313
mining as an engineer is pretty
well defined and it's pretty slow.

00:05:19.418 --> 00:05:22.580
It's sort of two years in
exhibition two in expedition and

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:27.228
everything about 15 years on operations in
the middle of nowhere because most mines

00:05:27.228 --> 00:05:30.018
don't happen to be on
San Francisco's doorstep.

00:05:30.018 --> 00:05:33.851
They tend to be in the middle of nowhere.

00:05:33.851 --> 00:05:38.115
And I wanted to get out of that, I didn't
want to have my career tied to being

00:05:38.115 --> 00:05:40.918
an operations for
the next 15 years of my life.

00:05:40.918 --> 00:05:45.780
And so I thought maybe it would
also help sort of expedite career

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.828
progression and give me more options.

00:05:48.828 --> 00:05:53.187
&gt;&gt; Yes, so
how did you come to know that it was time?

00:05:53.187 --> 00:05:56.590
Did you hear about an MBA from
other folks in your company?

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.713
Was this something you always
knew you wanted to do?

00:05:59.713 --> 00:06:01.714
How did know it was time?

00:06:01.714 --> 00:06:04.540
&gt;&gt; That's a great question, [LAUGH].

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.860
I don't know if there
is the right time to.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:12.094
I was on a sort of rotational
leadership program with

00:06:12.094 --> 00:06:16.245
a company I was working for,
which was five years.

00:06:16.245 --> 00:06:20.980
And I was coming to the end of
that program said about a year,

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.367
three and a half to year four.

00:06:23.367 --> 00:06:28.463
I sort of started having discussions
with some of my mentors,

00:06:28.463 --> 00:06:30.720
many of them had done MBAs.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.876
Many of the people who had been through
the program I was on before me had

00:06:34.876 --> 00:06:35.595
done MBAs.

00:06:35.595 --> 00:06:41.486
And it seemed a natural
progression from that program.

00:06:41.486 --> 00:06:44.446
You sort of got to see
a lot of different things,

00:06:44.446 --> 00:06:47.628
got to be working a bunch
of leadership positions.

00:06:47.628 --> 00:06:50.502
And then there was no real progression for
you in the company,

00:06:50.502 --> 00:06:52.558
there's no set if you're
going to go do this.

00:06:52.558 --> 00:06:56.938
And that's how I went to engineering
route, which was long and slow and

00:06:56.938 --> 00:06:58.700
I didn't really want to do.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:03.530
An MBA seem to open up many more doors,
so.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:07.008
&gt;&gt; The power that mentors can have
in your life is pretty incredible.

00:07:07.008 --> 00:07:12.218
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, I mean,
one of those mentors ended up writing one

00:07:12.218 --> 00:07:17.136
of my admission letters or
recommendation to [INAUDIBLE] and

00:07:17.136 --> 00:07:21.881
has been a really great mentor and
for sure, even still.

00:07:21.881 --> 00:07:23.095
&gt;&gt; Then Carl, how about you?

00:07:23.095 --> 00:07:27.669
You started in healthcare, why an MBA?

00:07:27.669 --> 00:07:32.380
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think if you had asked me that
five, seven years ago when I started med

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:36.310
school, I would have thought it
was a really weird career move.

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:40.527
But pretty early,
when I started my residency I got really

00:07:40.527 --> 00:07:44.065
frustrated about how
inefficient things were.

00:07:44.065 --> 00:07:48.573
Honestly patients waiting for
a bed in the emerge for a days.

00:07:48.573 --> 00:07:52.439
Surgeries getting cancelled,
improper resource allocation and

00:07:52.439 --> 00:07:56.856
realize that the people making those
decisions were either business people

00:07:56.856 --> 00:07:58.940
without a healthcare background.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:02.840
So without really the tools to understand
the implications of their decisions.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:06.120
Or healthcare people without
a business background.

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:09.709
So yeah,
they understand how doctors work but

00:08:09.709 --> 00:08:15.885
they don't really understand operations or
human resources or supply chain.

00:08:15.885 --> 00:08:18.735
All those things that are really
important when you're managing a large

00:08:18.735 --> 00:08:20.295
organization like a hospital.

00:08:20.295 --> 00:08:24.460
And so for me, it was really
a way to blend the two worlds and

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:28.965
to be able to bring back those
backgrounds into my industry to be

00:08:28.965 --> 00:08:32.540
the one making the decision someday.

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:36.963
&gt;&gt; And then for you, so was it the
exposure in your workplace that got you to

00:08:36.963 --> 00:08:38.970
feel like it was the right time?

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:43.932
Or how did you get exposed
to the MBA side of things?

00:08:43.932 --> 00:08:45.911
&gt;&gt; It was kind of a fluke almost.

00:08:45.911 --> 00:08:49.619
I think I thought about how can I
best get the that skill set and

00:08:49.619 --> 00:08:52.471
thought about a bunch
of different programs.

00:08:52.471 --> 00:08:56.960
I thought about maybe doing healthcare
administration, so be specific.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:00.005
I thought about doing public policy,
public health and

00:09:00.005 --> 00:09:03.127
thinking more about the interplay
with the government.

00:09:03.127 --> 00:09:07.306
And I ended up settling in on to the MBA
because I thought it was the more

00:09:07.306 --> 00:09:11.933
versatile degree and I would get to
meet people from different industries.

00:09:11.933 --> 00:09:14.413
Healthcare can be very
in bred at times and so

00:09:14.413 --> 00:09:18.395
we bring the same solution to
the same problems all the time.

00:09:18.395 --> 00:09:22.414
So it's meeting people from these
different industries who knew nothing

00:09:22.414 --> 00:09:27.095
about the hospital but knew lots of things
about mining or about consulting finance.

00:09:27.095 --> 00:09:30.749
I thought it would be a super exciting
opportunity to be exposed to all those

00:09:30.749 --> 00:09:32.205
different ways of thinking.

00:09:33.515 --> 00:09:38.404
&gt;&gt; So for both of you coming from mining,
coming from healthcare, Carl,

00:09:38.404 --> 00:09:42.804
you mentioned like almost coming
across this as a fluke Graham,

00:09:42.804 --> 00:09:47.550
you had some mentors coming
into the B school experience.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:53.011
As you were exploring your options,
as you were filling out your application,

00:09:53.011 --> 00:09:59.310
even, were there any stereotypes or kind
of notions you had about business school?

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:02.795
And then did any of them come
true how did that hand out?

00:10:02.795 --> 00:10:05.240
And so maybe Gram, let's start with you.

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:09.330
I think that mining background,
it's so fascinating

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:11.332
&gt;&gt; I think notions about business school

00:10:14.733 --> 00:10:18.491
I think growing up certainly
an undergrad and on my career,

00:10:18.491 --> 00:10:23.303
I think the notion what business school
is always probably people with a very

00:10:23.303 --> 00:10:28.265
good set of consulting background,
people who are in strategy and finance and

00:10:28.265 --> 00:10:31.379
running companies and
probably very much that.

00:10:31.379 --> 00:10:36.166
And then business school itself being
hours upon hours upon hours of reading

00:10:36.166 --> 00:10:40.290
cases and preparing cases for
class debates and stuff like that.

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:46.921
I think those are probably my
preconceived notions of business school.

00:10:46.921 --> 00:10:50.906
And so people running around and
in black suits and ties and

00:10:50.906 --> 00:10:55.544
having debates and discussing your
strategy companies would do and

00:10:55.544 --> 00:10:58.817
where they shouldn't
mess with you like that.

00:10:58.817 --> 00:10:59.735
None of that is true.

00:10:59.735 --> 00:11:02.780
&gt;&gt; So what did you experience?

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:08.396
How would you describe kind of your
experience with some of those notions?

00:11:08.396 --> 00:11:11.480
&gt;&gt; First I think the dress is very casual.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:15.760
People go to class in flip flops and
shorts and a T shirt.

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.720
And then on other days,
maybe something more extravagant.

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:26.779
And I think that just the breadth of what
is current at business school is not

00:11:26.779 --> 00:11:32.420
just discussing strategy and
finances and investment opportunities.

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:34.070
There's so much on soft skills.

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:36.340
There's so much on personal growth and
development.

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:39.177
There's so
much in different areas of the business.

00:11:39.177 --> 00:11:42.110
Be it some of it is strategy and
finances but

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:46.033
there's also marketing and
sales and human resources.

00:11:46.033 --> 00:11:50.569
There's just a lot, there's so
much that you can choose for and

00:11:50.569 --> 00:11:55.357
there's very specific courses or
if you wanted to focus on family,

00:11:55.357 --> 00:12:00.573
run businesses or a sort of focus on
healthcare specific classes on that.

00:12:00.573 --> 00:12:04.160
So you can dive into an industry,
you can dive into a particular area.

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:08.130
There's just so many different options.

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:12.480
And I think one thing that also
pretty stands out from me from

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:18.070
Stanford is how open people are to
discussing different ideas.

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:21.000
And it might be ideas
about a new business.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.326
It might be ideas about an investment, but

00:12:23.326 --> 00:12:27.090
it could also be an idea about
somewhere you want to change the world.

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:33.260
Some new nonprofit you want to start,
some new tech startup you want to do.

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:37.780
It wasn't just about running around
in suits and ties discussing options.

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:46.153
&gt;&gt; Then Carl, for you I think I might have
watched a few too many hospital TV shows

00:12:46.153 --> 00:12:52.700
where there is a very specific notion
of the business people in that world.

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:55.454
Did you have any preconceived notions or

00:12:55.454 --> 00:12:58.775
my preconceived notions
about that world true?

00:12:58.775 --> 00:13:01.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah, I definitely did.

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:06.081
I think my opinion of business school and
business students at the time was there

00:13:06.081 --> 00:13:10.410
people who are there because they
want to make a lot of money.

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:14.040
They don't necessarily care about the
impact they're going to have on the world.

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:17.130
It's going to be like all hard skills.

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:21.320
And there's going to be very little
dedicated to interpersonal skills.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:26.470
And it was completely the opposite and
I found specifically.

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:30.428
So at Stanford,
especially here, people were so

00:13:30.428 --> 00:13:36.665
focused on what their dream was, what
impact they wanted to have on the world.

00:13:36.665 --> 00:13:40.655
What they wanted to change,
and also that there was so

00:13:40.655 --> 00:13:45.532
many different classes that I took
that helped me become better at

00:13:45.532 --> 00:13:49.090
relating to other people
like communicating.

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:53.735
And those I use every day even in my
clinical work, it's so useful to have

00:13:53.735 --> 00:13:59.242
taken classes about how to break bad news
about how to have difficult conversations.

00:13:59.242 --> 00:14:04.008
And so that was like the complete
opposite of what I expected really.

00:14:04.008 --> 00:14:07.480
&gt;&gt; Yeah, nice.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:11.147
So we're still in this world of
approaching business school,

00:14:11.147 --> 00:14:13.581
you're maybe in the application cycle.

00:14:13.581 --> 00:14:18.500
There are a couple questions popping up
in the Q&amp;A box about telling your story.

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:24.787
Having come from a stem background myself,
I didn't know kind of what

00:14:24.787 --> 00:14:29.310
that meant in a kind of leadership
professional space setting.

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:33.589
So how did you start to
talk about your story and

00:14:33.589 --> 00:14:37.007
how did you approach that side of it?

00:14:37.007 --> 00:14:41.171
And so maybe, Carl, since you were
talking a little bit about some of those

00:14:41.171 --> 00:14:45.665
soft skills, and I think maybe even some
of your experiences at the GSB might have

00:14:45.665 --> 00:14:48.862
been on in how do you share your story and
support others.

00:14:48.862 --> 00:14:51.771
Can you tell us a little
bit more about that?

00:14:51.771 --> 00:14:53.120
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I was just very honest.

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:57.572
I pretty much said what I'm telling you
right now that I think business is evil,

00:14:57.572 --> 00:15:01.095
but I'm realizing more and
more that it's a necessary evil and

00:15:01.095 --> 00:15:04.902
that I need to learn those skills
because I want to help my patients.

00:15:04.902 --> 00:15:08.907
So talked a lot about what I do
the problems that I see day today,

00:15:08.907 --> 00:15:13.230
what I want to fix and how I think
the GSB is going to help me get there.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:18.320
So it was a lot of reflecting on
why is it that I want this degree?

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:20.650
What are the things that I
think I could do with it?

00:15:20.650 --> 00:15:24.394
Who do I think I could help and
how can the GSB get me there,

00:15:24.394 --> 00:15:28.770
and why is that the school
that I think can get me there?

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:33.289
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and for Gram, that reflective
element of the application process and

00:15:33.289 --> 00:15:37.460
telling your story, is that something
that resonates for you as well or

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:39.930
how did that take shape?

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:43.670
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think absolutely I
completely agree with Carl.

00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:47.886
When you think about your story,
which is not something as an engineer,

00:15:47.886 --> 00:15:49.486
we've never really done.

00:15:49.486 --> 00:15:52.946
You're kind of an engineer,
this is what you're going to do.

00:15:52.946 --> 00:15:54.470
There's no story.

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:58.541
I think that reflection
pieces could have go and

00:15:58.541 --> 00:16:03.401
figuring out What are you doing,
what is important to you?

00:16:03.401 --> 00:16:09.896
And very much a part of the application
process, what is most important to you?

00:16:09.896 --> 00:16:13.697
And then as you think about that,
I wouldn't go so

00:16:13.697 --> 00:16:19.322
far as to say I thought business was evil,
but it is certainly necessary.

00:16:19.322 --> 00:16:23.568
It was something I thought was
perhaps boring and simple compared to

00:16:23.568 --> 00:16:29.400
engineering problems but unnecessary
something that I knew nothing about.

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:33.880
And, I suppose from a business
perspective always thought the heart of

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:37.250
the business is in the side
that's doing this stuff.

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:42.079
If it's manufacturing, if it's mining,
if it's coding, it's the people building,

00:16:42.079 --> 00:16:43.930
it's the people creating stuff.

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:48.900
It's the operation side and
then there's the business side.

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:53.820
It just facilitates that and supports it.

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:59.326
Now, I suppose I think it does more but
I think it's important to understand

00:16:59.326 --> 00:17:04.760
both sides and really think about just
going back to thinking about story.

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:08.247
It's just understanding what you
want to get out of business school and

00:17:08.247 --> 00:17:10.385
who are you, what's important to you?.

00:17:10.385 --> 00:17:14.538
I think this part was important to know.

00:17:14.538 --> 00:17:16.755
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I love those questions.

00:17:16.755 --> 00:17:17.435
Who are you?.

00:17:17.435 --> 00:17:19.115
What's important to you?.

00:17:19.115 --> 00:17:21.075
What are you hoping to
get out of this process?.

00:17:22.705 --> 00:17:26.854
&gt;&gt; And kind of, just to add to that,
I think is also figuring out your story I

00:17:26.854 --> 00:17:29.725
think can be a bit of
an iterative process as well.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:34.750
So, you said, yeah,
I think this is what's important to me.

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:38.520
And then for me that had been
renewable energy part of that.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:42.974
And in my first year, I went to did an
internship in renewable energy company and

00:17:42.974 --> 00:17:47.510
figured out what that is like and tried
an earlier stage startup and tested that.

00:17:47.510 --> 00:17:50.710
And then you sort of come back and
say, okay, how does this fit my story?

00:17:50.710 --> 00:17:52.870
Don't update it now,
I'm going to try something else.

00:17:54.275 --> 00:17:55.420
That's it.

00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:59.010
And that's, I think, a big part
of the purpose almost of an MBA.

00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:02.896
And the benefit is you get
to figure out your story.

00:18:02.896 --> 00:18:06.799
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there's a question here,
Graham from Priscilla,

00:18:06.799 --> 00:18:12.273
who has a career in construction which is,
some similar to mining and a lot of ways.

00:18:12.273 --> 00:18:16.661
And how do you talk about,
quote unquote fit for

00:18:16.661 --> 00:18:21.716
an MBA coming from to use her words,
a harsh industry.

00:18:21.716 --> 00:18:29.316
How did you approach
kind of that side of it?

00:18:29.316 --> 00:18:32.300
&gt;&gt; I think that might depend on different
business schools and the culture.

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:35.100
I think it really depends on
the culture of the school.

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:38.680
I think Stanford is a very open culture.

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:42.904
The same time it's a very non harsh or
hard skills,

00:18:42.904 --> 00:18:47.423
culture in the sense that I
think the first time I taught

00:18:47.423 --> 00:18:52.357
someone else from mining,
they asked me if I made Bitcoin.

00:18:52.357 --> 00:18:58.357
No, actual be in the gram,
and when you think about fit,

00:18:58.357 --> 00:19:03.357
it runs stories fits in in some way or
other way,

00:19:03.357 --> 00:19:08.739
it runs background as well in some way or
other way.

00:19:08.739 --> 00:19:12.520
It might not be in every case in
every class in every discussion.

00:19:12.520 --> 00:19:14.991
And I think you figure out,

00:19:14.991 --> 00:19:20.160
it's part of figuring out your
story is where do I fit in?

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:22.260
Where can I contribute?

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:26.880
And so for me that was not
in the accounting classroom.

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:30.747
But there might have been other
classrooms on operations or

00:19:30.747 --> 00:19:34.800
optimization was like,
okay, this is great event.

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:37.751
Or there are cases on perhaps
in a mining company so

00:19:37.751 --> 00:19:39.940
when I get it I can give context here.

00:19:41.770 --> 00:19:47.800
But then even outside of that, your
background is only one part of your story.

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:50.945
There's also where you want to go
is another part of your story, and

00:19:50.945 --> 00:19:53.080
how can you contribute there?

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:56.100
How can you so for me,
I wanted to go to renewable energy.

00:19:56.100 --> 00:20:00.500
So I did the IPA, I joined all the energy
classes, I joined the energy club.

00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:03.618
And then I was probably learn more for

00:20:03.618 --> 00:20:08.050
things in energy then for
my background and mining.

00:20:11.060 --> 00:20:15.901
And so it's just a matter of figuring
out what's important to you and

00:20:15.901 --> 00:20:17.830
then communicating that.

00:20:19.030 --> 00:20:22.696
And even though mining
wasn't that important to me,

00:20:22.696 --> 00:20:25.550
every time anything about money came up.

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:30.766
Everyone got pointed in my ear
towards me and said, go talk to him.

00:20:30.766 --> 00:20:35.118
I had professors who say, professors
fashioned versus saying are they

00:20:35.118 --> 00:20:38.249
interested in the mining space and
renewable energy.

00:20:38.249 --> 00:20:41.190
And they would set up coffee chats for
me to come talk to me

00:20:41.190 --> 00:20:45.386
then that introduced me to other people
in the broader both Stanford network and

00:20:45.386 --> 00:20:48.788
even Palo Alto network of people
who are also working in mining.

00:20:48.788 --> 00:20:56.103
And said, his rare mining MBA person,
talk to them.

00:20:56.103 --> 00:21:00.490
So if you figure out what's important
to you, and you communicate that,

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:05.161
I think you find your fit and you'll find
the people with similar interests and

00:21:05.161 --> 00:21:08.243
you'll find the people you
should be speaking to.

00:21:08.243 --> 00:21:10.222
I hope that answers the question.

00:21:10.222 --> 00:21:12.940
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that answered it beautifully, and

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:16.490
it pops up a follow up question I have for
Carl.

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:20.830
So Graham talked a little bit about some
of the contributions his perspective

00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:25.503
contributed either in classroom community
and now kind of up broader scale of folks

00:21:25.503 --> 00:21:29.441
who might've been interested in
are looking for someone to talk to and

00:21:29.441 --> 00:21:30.530
learn more about.

00:21:30.530 --> 00:21:34.787
What are some of the ways that you've
saw you've seen your pre MBA background

00:21:34.787 --> 00:21:39.190
contribute or add value to either
the classroom or conversations you've had?

00:21:41.130 --> 00:21:43.785
&gt;&gt; Well, I think there's
the obvious thing about like,

00:21:43.785 --> 00:21:47.306
whenever healthcare pops up in a class
like people would all turn to me and

00:21:47.306 --> 00:21:50.616
be like, Carl, what do you think
like is that is that how this goes?

00:21:50.616 --> 00:21:56.003
Like we literally had a case in
our optimization class about or

00:21:56.003 --> 00:21:59.740
scheduling at a hospital I had worked out.

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:02.790
And we had another one about how
to optimize the processes in

00:22:02.790 --> 00:22:04.010
an anesthesia clinic.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:08.549
So it was very easy for me to be like, so
these are how these things would happen.

00:22:08.549 --> 00:22:13.541
But even beyond that,
I think a lot of the soft skills portions

00:22:13.541 --> 00:22:17.867
like those are things that
I have gotten to practice.

00:22:17.867 --> 00:22:20.779
And will get to practice more every day

00:22:20.779 --> 00:22:25.520
like telling people that their
loved one is dying or has died.

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:28.321
Like those are very
difficult conversations and

00:22:28.321 --> 00:22:32.770
there's a lot of parallels that you can
make between that and firing people.

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:36.145
Or telling them that the company
is going bankrupt or

00:22:36.145 --> 00:22:40.375
making those big announcements
when you're a business leader.

00:22:40.375 --> 00:22:45.164
And I think that I brought that
unique lens to those conversations

00:22:45.164 --> 00:22:48.570
that added a lot to
the conversations really.

00:22:48.570 --> 00:22:54.531
&gt;&gt; Nice, so, as we're talking about the
GSB and the business school experience,

00:22:54.531 --> 00:22:59.980
let's start from the beginning of that,
from when classes started.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:02.480
And this is a question
from the team out there.

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:11.130
Did you feel behind in classes coming
from a non-business background?

00:23:11.130 --> 00:23:15.555
What was that transition into
the business classroom like for you guys?

00:23:15.555 --> 00:23:20.299
So maybe Carl, we'll start with you and
then Graham, we'll hop over to you next.

00:23:20.299 --> 00:23:25.249
&gt;&gt; Yeah, its something I'm worried
about a little before starting

00:23:25.249 --> 00:23:28.314
knowing that I had the math in 10 year.

00:23:28.314 --> 00:23:32.074
Knew virtually nothing
about accounting finance,

00:23:32.074 --> 00:23:37.028
but for other classes you can think
of when you are thinking about your

00:23:37.028 --> 00:23:41.317
first year business students is taking but
I will say that.

00:23:41.317 --> 00:23:45.526
They're aware that people come
in with different levels and

00:23:45.526 --> 00:23:48.426
so a lot of the intro classes are tearing.

00:23:48.426 --> 00:23:53.269
To make sure that you enter in at
the right level and you make sure that you

00:23:53.269 --> 00:23:57.880
learn what you need to learn but
also that everybody is stimulated.

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:00.790
The other thing I think is that

00:24:04.732 --> 00:24:09.445
Coming from an MD background I had
already done graduate studies and

00:24:09.445 --> 00:24:12.515
already had a sense of learning for
myself.

00:24:12.515 --> 00:24:15.863
And I for what I need to learn and
for what I need for

00:24:15.863 --> 00:24:19.966
my career versus learning in
order to get specific grades.

00:24:19.966 --> 00:24:24.285
And so I think I came into the program
putting very little pressure on myself and

00:24:24.285 --> 00:24:26.911
being, like,
I'm going to take as much as I can.

00:24:26.911 --> 00:24:31.035
But if there's this one piece of
information I can't memorize it for

00:24:31.035 --> 00:24:32.180
whatever reason.

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:36.058
And I think there's something I
can do with my time that would

00:24:36.058 --> 00:24:40.638
add more value to this experience,
then that's what I'm going to do.

00:24:40.638 --> 00:24:45.706
And I think that really helped me get
through with a minimal amounts of stress

00:24:45.706 --> 00:24:50.473
compared to some other people who
didn't have that net view coming in.

00:24:50.473 --> 00:24:52.973
&gt;&gt; Yes, and then Graham, how about you?

00:24:52.973 --> 00:24:57.241
How was that transition coming from
a non business background into

00:24:57.241 --> 00:24:58.925
the business classroom?

00:24:58.925 --> 00:25:03.970
&gt;&gt; I think the key thing is Karachi
product is knowing what you want

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:09.697
to get out of it, and where you need
to put your time and attention to it.

00:25:09.697 --> 00:25:14.634
And so if you're coming from
a non-digital background,

00:25:14.634 --> 00:25:18.978
you say you already wanted to and
finances, okay?

00:25:18.978 --> 00:25:24.144
Then you know you need to put your
attention into accounting and

00:25:24.144 --> 00:25:28.540
econ and finance one on one and
ensure those classes.

00:25:28.540 --> 00:25:31.113
Well, they take up more of your time, but

00:25:31.113 --> 00:25:36.206
because you know where you want to put
your attention, I think that really helps.

00:25:36.206 --> 00:25:41.877
And as Colin mentioned, the highlight
to the classes is the intro classes or

00:25:41.877 --> 00:25:44.899
the first sort of quarter is really key.

00:25:44.899 --> 00:25:49.691
Because then my first year accounting
class was everyone else who had

00:25:49.691 --> 00:25:52.429
never done accounting before either.

00:25:52.429 --> 00:25:56.254
Or the people who were accountants
were in the third advanced or

00:25:56.254 --> 00:25:58.356
the level three class basically.

00:25:58.356 --> 00:26:01.987
And they were discussing
some super advanced stuff,

00:26:01.987 --> 00:26:05.141
I don't even know what
they were discussing.

00:26:05.141 --> 00:26:10.025
But they weren't in our class, they're
being bored or asking questions that

00:26:10.025 --> 00:26:14.025
are just so far above my head that
I have no idea what's going on.

00:26:14.025 --> 00:26:16.526
So I think that's really critical.

00:26:16.526 --> 00:26:20.092
I think the other element
to the transition

00:26:20.092 --> 00:26:24.561
is just being back in a classroom and
having to study.

00:26:24.561 --> 00:26:29.641
And just paying back in the classroom
if I hadn't done that for

00:26:29.641 --> 00:26:34.538
five years, and that is a bit
of a shock to the system again.

00:26:34.538 --> 00:26:36.588
Yeah, I think that's
something to be aware of.

00:26:36.588 --> 00:26:40.162
But again,
I think the first quarter allows for

00:26:40.162 --> 00:26:46.251
that transition instead of read the whole
first quarter sort of ramps up as well.

00:26:46.251 --> 00:26:51.299
Under without doing any sports or
the activities where they're on hold and

00:26:51.299 --> 00:26:54.915
go participate in those I
think really helps as well.

00:26:54.915 --> 00:27:00.161
&gt;&gt; Then Graham there's a couple
questions in the Q&amp;A box about like EI4,

00:27:00.161 --> 00:27:04.119
did you know you wanted to do
the the program coming in?

00:27:04.119 --> 00:27:08.907
And what was it like with
the two programs, like,

00:27:08.907 --> 00:27:11.989
what was that experience like?

00:27:11.989 --> 00:27:15.150
&gt;&gt; So I did know I want to
do the program coming in.

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:21.791
I happen to have a small group dinner
I think my first week at Stanford,

00:27:21.791 --> 00:27:26.085
which was hosted by
a secondary year person.

00:27:26.085 --> 00:27:27.140
And two of one and

00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:31.984
there was also two classmates of mine
who are already in the active program.

00:27:31.984 --> 00:27:36.148
And they sort of joined before or
as I joined the MBA, and

00:27:36.148 --> 00:27:40.414
we just got talking about that,
and it sounded awesome.

00:27:40.414 --> 00:27:44.651
And I think the next day I
filled out the application.

00:27:44.651 --> 00:27:46.731
So that is more by chance, but

00:27:46.731 --> 00:27:52.249
I definitely think it's worthwhile
looking into before if you know about it.

00:27:52.249 --> 00:27:55.821
And then in terms of
managing the two programs,

00:27:55.821 --> 00:27:59.580
it's very flexible in terms
of how you manage it.

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:03.709
I think there's a slightly higher
overall points requirement,

00:28:03.709 --> 00:28:09.950
I can't remember exactly it's imagine 20,
240 or 100, 120 or something like that.

00:28:09.950 --> 00:28:14.598
But you can do it anywhere from
an extra one quarter to an extra three

00:28:14.598 --> 00:28:16.261
quarters at Stanford.

00:28:16.261 --> 00:28:20.187
So you can really decide if
you want to almost, like,

00:28:20.187 --> 00:28:25.084
sort of do 20 units every quarter for
the two years aone quarter.

00:28:25.084 --> 00:28:29.469
You can finish it quickly in
just one additional quarter and

00:28:29.469 --> 00:28:31.934
you will be busy but you can do it.

00:28:31.934 --> 00:28:34.202
If you want to take a much
more leisurely pace for

00:28:34.202 --> 00:28:37.735
the whole thing you can do three years
to do both the MBA and the undergrad.

00:28:37.735 --> 00:28:41.890
And then have maybe one year being
busy and one year medium and

00:28:41.890 --> 00:28:44.346
one really, really relaxed year.

00:28:44.346 --> 00:28:47.097
You can have three pretty relaxed years,

00:28:47.097 --> 00:28:49.853
you can really split
it up how you want to.

00:28:49.853 --> 00:28:55.315
And managing that is up to you really and
once again,

00:28:55.315 --> 00:29:00.791
what's important and
what you want to get out of it.

00:29:00.791 --> 00:29:05.454
The most challenging
part is probably managing

00:29:05.454 --> 00:29:09.424
the non-GSB classes that all courses.

00:29:09.424 --> 00:29:13.538
Because some of those will occur one
year and they won't occur again or

00:29:13.538 --> 00:29:15.150
they'll occur two years.

00:29:15.150 --> 00:29:18.888
And then you budgeting on doing it in
the next year and it disappears or

00:29:18.888 --> 00:29:21.089
they're only often in spring quarter.

00:29:21.089 --> 00:29:24.688
So you got to try to budget that you're
going to do in spring quarter and

00:29:24.688 --> 00:29:27.799
then manage conflicts with other
GSB classes you want to do.

00:29:27.799 --> 00:29:32.607
So it's really about managing
the schedule of what's available when and

00:29:32.607 --> 00:29:35.408
the conflicts that might occur from that.

00:29:35.408 --> 00:29:38.154
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a little bit more logistics,

00:29:38.154 --> 00:29:41.291
a little bit of pre-planning and
come through.

00:29:41.291 --> 00:29:48.099
&gt;&gt; But there's great support for both GSB
and IPAR to help you manage that process.

00:29:48.099 --> 00:29:50.792
And they are very accommodating and

00:29:50.792 --> 00:29:55.561
flexible if you just talk to them and
start discussions early.

00:29:55.561 --> 00:30:01.419
&gt;&gt; So continuing on the theme of things
you did while you're at the GSB,

00:30:01.419 --> 00:30:06.573
Carl, you went from health care
went back into health care.

00:30:06.573 --> 00:30:11.319
Graham you went from mining and
then you went into consulting, so

00:30:11.319 --> 00:30:14.091
we have two different journeys here.

00:30:14.091 --> 00:30:19.501
So Clara, if I can start with you,
there's a question here

00:30:19.501 --> 00:30:25.035
in the inbox from Luis about,
wait, that's the wrong one.

00:30:25.035 --> 00:30:28.654
Sorry, there is a question here about,

00:30:28.654 --> 00:30:32.703
why did you choose Stanford for
healthcare?

00:30:32.703 --> 00:30:34.761
&gt;&gt; There's a bunch of reasons,

00:30:34.761 --> 00:30:39.134
I think one of it is just
the excellence of the hospital system.

00:30:39.134 --> 00:30:44.011
And I knew that I could meet a lot of
people working within it, who I could see

00:30:44.011 --> 00:30:49.369
as contacts, both within the business-side
and within the healthcare-side.

00:30:49.369 --> 00:30:53.452
I did, for example,
do a quality improvement project with

00:30:53.452 --> 00:30:56.485
Stanford Healthcare
while I was at the GSB.

00:30:56.485 --> 00:31:02.706
The other reason which is kind of around
about way to go about health care but

00:31:02.706 --> 00:31:08.437
this strong emphasis that Stanford
puts on interpersonal skills and

00:31:08.437 --> 00:31:10.725
on communication skills.

00:31:10.725 --> 00:31:14.036
And those are so
key in healthcare and I was, like,

00:31:14.036 --> 00:31:17.963
even if I go into this degree and
decide that I hate business,

00:31:17.963 --> 00:31:21.359
I never want to do it,
I never want to manage people.

00:31:21.359 --> 00:31:25.930
I'm going to need to manage
relationships with patients and

00:31:25.930 --> 00:31:30.137
I'm going to need to manage
difficult conversations.

00:31:30.137 --> 00:31:34.109
And those are things that Stanford
is going to teach me no matter what.

00:31:34.109 --> 00:31:38.769
And so I think that was the other
thing that really attracted me to

00:31:38.769 --> 00:31:42.146
Stanford as someone
coming from healthcare.

00:31:42.146 --> 00:31:46.571
I did take a lot of classes at the Med
school as well during my two years,

00:31:46.571 --> 00:31:48.527
which I thought was a big plus.

00:31:48.527 --> 00:31:52.627
The ability to take classes kind
of everywhere on campus was

00:31:52.627 --> 00:31:54.106
very exciting to me.

00:31:54.106 --> 00:31:58.156
And took a bunch of the the healthcare
electives that were offered at Stanford.

00:31:58.156 --> 00:32:02.219
And really enjoyed them and learned a lot
about how the healthcare industry is

00:32:02.219 --> 00:32:03.983
structured in the US and abroad.

00:32:03.983 --> 00:32:07.790
And how to Manage large healthcare
organizations, what are the different

00:32:07.790 --> 00:32:11.530
experiments that have been done
on the business side about that?

00:32:11.530 --> 00:32:13.510
Those were all super cool experiences for
me.

00:32:14.570 --> 00:32:17.724
&gt;&gt; So, question, so I'm going to
follow up a little bit here and

00:32:17.724 --> 00:32:19.500
adapt the question from Daniel.

00:32:19.500 --> 00:32:24.421
So that original question was from Nina,
so, sorry, I got names mixed up Nina.

00:32:24.421 --> 00:32:29.524
But Daniel's asking a little bit
about transformation about moments or

00:32:29.524 --> 00:32:33.382
an experience you have that
helped you change in a way.

00:32:33.382 --> 00:32:36.935
How do you think you've changed
even if your career trajectory and

00:32:36.935 --> 00:32:38.908
your career goal remained the same?

00:32:38.908 --> 00:32:43.217
What are some of the ways you've
seen yourself kind of grow or

00:32:43.217 --> 00:32:46.270
expand from your time at business school?

00:32:46.270 --> 00:32:49.938
&gt;&gt; I think Stanford gives
you a lot of opportunity for

00:32:49.938 --> 00:32:52.126
feedback from other people.

00:32:52.126 --> 00:32:56.287
It's one of the things that we're known
for that we even make jokes about.

00:32:56.287 --> 00:33:00.022
We give each other feedback
all the time and it's a gift.

00:33:00.022 --> 00:33:02.212
And so I think very early on,

00:33:02.212 --> 00:33:07.142
I was told something that had never
been told in health care, and

00:33:07.142 --> 00:33:11.910
that's that I'm not very
emotionally expressive, [LAUGH].

00:33:11.910 --> 00:33:16.726
But that sometimes means that
people take a bit of time to

00:33:16.726 --> 00:33:20.012
figure out whether or not I like them.

00:33:20.012 --> 00:33:24.928
And I got a million other little pieces
of feedback about how I interact

00:33:24.928 --> 00:33:26.519
with other people on.

00:33:26.519 --> 00:33:31.454
And thinking about those,
and trying to work on those,

00:33:31.454 --> 00:33:35.864
and trying to find strategies
that work for me to,

00:33:35.864 --> 00:33:41.329
I don't want to say overcome because
that's not really a default.

00:33:41.329 --> 00:33:44.715
But to work around those
little quirks that I have,

00:33:44.715 --> 00:33:49.465
have been really, really helpful in
how I interact with other people.

00:33:49.465 --> 00:33:51.627
I was stuck in an apartment
with my sister for

00:33:51.627 --> 00:33:54.613
three months at the beginning
of quarantine, for example.

00:33:54.613 --> 00:33:59.056
And I was using some of the tricks
that I learned in interpersonal

00:33:59.056 --> 00:34:03.758
dynamics every single day to prevent
us from murdering each other.

00:34:03.758 --> 00:34:07.040
[LAUGH] because sometimes
it got a little heating.

00:34:09.170 --> 00:34:11.925
&gt;&gt; Yes, and then Graham, you pivoted,

00:34:11.925 --> 00:34:15.980
did you know that you wanted
to go into consulting?

00:34:15.980 --> 00:34:18.089
Or how did you kind of,

00:34:18.089 --> 00:34:23.431
how did you know that you were
going to go in that direction?

00:34:23.431 --> 00:34:28.031
&gt;&gt; So I didn't know,
I will say that in my application,

00:34:28.031 --> 00:34:33.261
I said I wanted go into consulting
to learn business skills.

00:34:33.261 --> 00:34:37.327
And this sort of gets back
to iterating on your story.

00:34:37.327 --> 00:34:41.912
So coming in, I thought this
sounds like a good story to tell.

00:34:41.912 --> 00:34:45.890
And it made sense to me and it made sense
for how I saw my career progressing.

00:34:47.170 --> 00:34:52.516
Then I joined Stanford decided I
want to go more into renewables and

00:34:52.516 --> 00:34:55.570
energy and joined the.

00:34:55.570 --> 00:34:58.807
And as I can't forget
this consulting thing,

00:34:58.807 --> 00:35:02.625
let me go into clean energy startup,
can you try that?

00:35:02.625 --> 00:35:08.491
Did that, just covered that startups,
depending very much on the startup or

00:35:08.491 --> 00:35:12.020
not necessarily always
a great place to work.

00:35:14.600 --> 00:35:18.370
And then sort of looked at,
okay, I've tried that.

00:35:18.370 --> 00:35:20.410
Where do I see myself growing now?

00:35:20.410 --> 00:35:24.460
And I actually came back
to the idea of consulting.

00:35:26.430 --> 00:35:29.890
Because of, again,
it's almost an extension of an MBA.

00:35:31.570 --> 00:35:38.010
Just learning skills, prosecutor
progression, opening more doors.

00:35:38.010 --> 00:35:44.939
But then also just because I do enjoys
sort of solving different problems.

00:35:44.939 --> 00:35:47.657
And consulting promised
to be a lot of that.

00:35:47.657 --> 00:35:52.472
&gt;&gt; Yeah, how did you explore the startup
space, was this during the GSB, or

00:35:52.472 --> 00:35:54.423
was this immediately after?

00:35:54.423 --> 00:35:57.769
&gt;&gt; This was during the GSB.

00:35:57.769 --> 00:36:02.784
So it was a lot of discussions with
some classmates even who had worked

00:36:02.784 --> 00:36:07.810
in startups, who had their own
startups we are starting something.

00:36:07.810 --> 00:36:13.503
It was with other alumni or
other people within the sort

00:36:13.503 --> 00:36:18.456
of energy startup ecosystem
that classmates or

00:36:18.456 --> 00:36:23.301
the Stanford community
helped connect me to.

00:36:23.301 --> 00:36:26.185
And then speaking to them and

00:36:26.185 --> 00:36:31.602
then sort of eventually picking one and
going for it.

00:36:31.602 --> 00:36:35.842
So definitely be part of
exploring at the GSB.

00:36:35.842 --> 00:36:42.720
&gt;&gt; And then same question from Daniel for
you about transformation and change.

00:36:42.720 --> 00:36:48.399
Is there a moment or
experience that has helped

00:36:48.399 --> 00:36:53.503
you kind of grow in some way shape or
form?

00:36:53.503 --> 00:37:00.884
&gt;&gt; Currently that is a singular moment
where I had moment that I think,

00:37:00.884 --> 00:37:04.766
but I think I just grew a lot of GSB and

00:37:04.766 --> 00:37:09.963
really from people's diverse perspectives.

00:37:09.963 --> 00:37:14.222
And I think it helped me
become even more open and

00:37:14.222 --> 00:37:21.586
aware of how I interact with people with
different backgrounds and experiences.

00:37:21.586 --> 00:37:25.209
And I've thought that growing up in
South Africa and dealing with a sort of on

00:37:25.209 --> 00:37:27.995
a continuous basis that I was
already pretty good at this.

00:37:27.995 --> 00:37:32.571
And then at the GSB, I think it just,

00:37:32.571 --> 00:37:38.044
I grew so
much particularly in that regard.

00:37:38.044 --> 00:37:39.190
And that was again,

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:42.436
the class interpersonal
dynamics that Carl mentioned.

00:37:42.436 --> 00:37:46.818
Also as touchy feeling, something
might hurt that class was incredible.

00:37:46.818 --> 00:37:49.749
But just the general discussions,

00:37:49.749 --> 00:37:55.817
I think it would really help to break
down any stereotypes you might have.

00:37:55.817 --> 00:37:58.923
Because you meet people
who just did not fit,

00:37:58.923 --> 00:38:03.258
whatever stereotype you had about
someone with some bad code or

00:38:03.258 --> 00:38:08.033
anything could be somebody completely
shattered that stereotype.

00:38:08.033 --> 00:38:11.025
And it just made me question
a lot of stereotypes I had.

00:38:11.025 --> 00:38:15.831
And then just that event,
you say you know what, this is just not

00:38:15.831 --> 00:38:21.177
helping me having these stereotypes,
let me just try to forget those.

00:38:21.177 --> 00:38:26.040
And I think that for me has been key.

00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:28.588
&gt;&gt; I have a question here from a Matthias,

00:38:28.588 --> 00:38:32.909
so we've kind of run the gauntlet
from kind of where you came from,

00:38:32.909 --> 00:38:37.384
healthcare and mining,
approaching the process, that transition

00:38:37.384 --> 00:38:42.109
between classes career exploration or
acceleration as the case may be.

00:38:42.109 --> 00:38:44.679
This question is from Matthias,

00:38:44.679 --> 00:38:48.991
how do you compare yourself
post to pre MBA at Stanford,

00:38:48.991 --> 00:38:54.700
if you had to put it in a few words,
so go ahead and take a few moments.

00:38:54.700 --> 00:38:58.490
Graham you just responded, so
maybe Carl, we'll start with you.

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:01.960
How would you describe yourself?

00:39:01.960 --> 00:39:08.996
How would you compare yourself
pre versus post Stanford MBA?

00:39:08.996 --> 00:39:10.757
&gt;&gt; I think it was a few things,

00:39:10.757 --> 00:39:15.200
the one thing is it's really expanded
my idea of what's possible and

00:39:15.200 --> 00:39:20.425
what are the different things I could do
and the different directions I could go.

00:39:20.425 --> 00:39:26.699
I think one quick example is the power
of just asking for what you want.

00:39:26.699 --> 00:39:28.775
That's something that the GSB taught me.

00:39:28.775 --> 00:39:31.653
So in the fall quarter of my second year,

00:39:31.653 --> 00:39:37.156
I emailed four different hospital CEOs
in Toronto, where I live currently,

00:39:37.156 --> 00:39:43.100
and just told them my story, told them,
look, basically I want your job someday.

00:39:43.100 --> 00:39:46.406
Would you mind getting on the phone for
20, 30 minutes and

00:39:46.406 --> 00:39:49.760
telling me how you got there and
what do I do to do this?

00:39:49.760 --> 00:39:53.605
And I don't think that's something
I would have had the courage or

00:39:53.605 --> 00:39:56.750
even thought about doing before the GSB.

00:39:56.750 --> 00:40:01.260
And they all responded, they all said yes,
they all got on the phone with me.

00:40:01.260 --> 00:40:04.906
So I learned a ton from those
conversations and just knowing that

00:40:04.906 --> 00:40:09.510
that's something I can do, I think was
a really big learning experience for me.

00:40:43.420 --> 00:40:47.307
Deliberate board member and can ask much
better questions just because I have

00:40:47.307 --> 00:40:49.800
a better sense of how
organizations are run.

00:40:49.800 --> 00:40:53.260
I learned Spanish while
I was there as well.

00:40:53.260 --> 00:40:57.446
So I've had a few patients who have
greatly benefited from that because they

00:40:57.446 --> 00:41:00.656
had a couple who were just
lingual Spanish speakers and

00:41:00.656 --> 00:41:05.500
got to give them their treatment in their
first language, which was pretty cool.

00:41:07.694 --> 00:41:08.804
&gt;&gt; And Graham, what about you?

00:41:08.804 --> 00:41:15.128
How would you describe
yourself pre versus post MBA?

00:41:15.128 --> 00:41:17.830
&gt;&gt; So I think I was bowling
off what I said before.

00:41:17.830 --> 00:41:22.459
I think I'm much more open to different

00:41:22.459 --> 00:41:26.668
perspectives, different ideas,

00:41:26.668 --> 00:41:32.350
aware of my own biases and
how I come across.

00:41:32.350 --> 00:41:36.170
And then better able to work with
other people and like I said,

00:41:36.170 --> 00:41:37.790
I communicate with them.

00:41:40.356 --> 00:41:43.689
I think much more aware of how,

00:41:43.689 --> 00:41:50.070
I think emotions play a role
in relationships as well.

00:41:50.070 --> 00:41:54.706
I had always sort of seen showing
vulnerability and asking for

00:41:54.706 --> 00:41:57.000
help as a weakness.

00:41:57.000 --> 00:42:01.565
And I think Stanford really helped me
to see how those can be beneficial and

00:42:01.565 --> 00:42:05.920
how they can be really actually
critical to building relationships.

00:42:08.750 --> 00:42:14.019
Similarly then also just a lot of
the hard skills and I feel I might not

00:42:14.019 --> 00:42:19.570
be an expert in accounting already
balance sheets or human resources,

00:42:19.570 --> 00:42:25.506
but I could have a meaningful discussion
in any of those areas if I needed to.

00:42:25.506 --> 00:42:27.956
And I sort of know what to ask,

00:42:27.956 --> 00:42:32.860
what the critical things are and
how to dive deep into that.

00:42:32.860 --> 00:42:37.840
And then also importantly know that
it's okay to ask for help and it's okay.

00:42:37.840 --> 00:42:42.220
I can have an entry level discussion to
this, but I can't go deep who to speak to.

00:42:42.220 --> 00:42:46.740
You can help me with this and
being comfortable to ask for that help.

00:43:08.300 --> 00:43:11.480
&gt;&gt; Is there anything that
you would do differently?

00:43:11.480 --> 00:43:15.080
So Graham, maybe we ca start with you.

00:43:15.080 --> 00:43:21.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would be more intentional
about how I spend my time.

00:43:22.540 --> 00:43:26.826
I think there is the searching or

00:43:26.826 --> 00:43:34.750
it is a bit of a culture but
it's not a necessarily real.

00:43:34.750 --> 00:43:38.610
So it's a perception
of grace don't matter.

00:43:38.610 --> 00:43:39.880
Academics don't matter.

00:43:40.940 --> 00:43:43.530
We're here to have fun and
like socialize and network.

00:43:44.710 --> 00:43:50.820
And while that's sure,
I think I took it almost too literally.

00:43:50.820 --> 00:43:53.416
And I think there's more
that I could have learned,

00:43:53.416 --> 00:43:58.000
which I would have liked to have learned
if I was more intentional about it.

00:43:58.000 --> 00:44:02.748
At the same time, I think I would
have changed my strategy of forming

00:44:02.748 --> 00:44:07.250
more meaningful connections with
maybe fewer people as opposed

00:44:07.250 --> 00:44:11.270
to focusing on stronger
connections with fewer people.

00:44:13.600 --> 00:44:16.460
And so I would have probably
changed those two approaches a bit.

00:44:17.640 --> 00:44:20.040
But I was not too significant yet.

00:44:21.180 --> 00:44:25.327
I think that just being really intentional
about how you spend your time is the most

00:44:25.327 --> 00:44:28.590
important thing and now I would
be more intentional about that.

00:44:30.260 --> 00:44:31.555
&gt;&gt; Cole what about yourself?

00:44:31.555 --> 00:44:35.937
&gt;&gt; It's almost a joke, but I think I
would do it completely differently,

00:44:35.937 --> 00:44:37.769
do everything differently.

00:44:37.769 --> 00:44:42.300
Just so I could get an experience
that's just nothing like what I had.

00:44:42.300 --> 00:44:44.417
I love the time I had that there's just so

00:44:44.417 --> 00:44:48.670
many opportunities that come with
being at Stanford in the MBA program.

00:44:48.670 --> 00:44:50.861
And there's always the sense that like,

00:44:50.861 --> 00:44:54.625
whenever you're doing something
you're missing out on 10 others.

00:44:54.625 --> 00:44:59.029
And so like just going back and open that
door that I didn't open, like maybe,

00:44:59.029 --> 00:45:01.880
I don't know, learn how to code.

00:45:01.880 --> 00:45:04.709
I go on that trip that I
didn't go to get to meet

00:45:04.709 --> 00:45:07.468
the other people that
I didn't get to meet.

00:45:07.468 --> 00:45:11.110
Lean and really hard on the like
hard finance skills and

00:45:11.110 --> 00:45:15.160
see where that gets me and
if that gets me somewhere different

00:45:17.230 --> 00:45:20.145
&gt;&gt; Were there any like classes or programs

00:45:20.145 --> 00:45:25.812
specifically that you would go back and
try again if you had a second chance?

00:45:25.812 --> 00:45:29.260
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I think I would try to learn how to code.

00:45:29.260 --> 00:45:31.250
I forget exactly what
class code those are.

00:45:31.250 --> 00:45:35.557
But they're across the street
classes about how to use like R and

00:45:35.557 --> 00:45:39.722
Python and I think it would be
very interesting skill to have.

00:45:39.722 --> 00:45:44.319
Plus, there's a lot of things that you can
do with it in any industry you go into, so

00:45:44.319 --> 00:45:47.000
maybe that's one thing that would try.

00:45:47.000 --> 00:45:51.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think you might be
referring to CS106 or CS105.

00:45:51.180 --> 00:45:52.960
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:45:52.960 --> 00:45:56.279
&gt;&gt; Is it to [LAUGH] the known
classes on campus for

00:45:56.279 --> 00:45:59.780
being in the computer science space.

00:45:59.780 --> 00:46:00.970
&gt;&gt; Yes, exactly those.

00:46:02.490 --> 00:46:05.788
&gt;&gt; Graham, I'm curious if there
was a class or program or

00:46:05.788 --> 00:46:10.320
something that you would try if you had
a second chance at Business School.

00:46:11.950 --> 00:46:15.357
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I really like cause approach of
saying try something completely new and

00:46:15.357 --> 00:46:17.590
different and
deliver the same experience twice.

00:46:17.590 --> 00:46:18.698
I think that's great.

00:46:18.698 --> 00:46:22.910
[LAUGH] And I suppose to that I
would have probably leaned in more

00:46:22.910 --> 00:46:25.020
on some of the heavy finances.

00:46:25.020 --> 00:46:31.114
So, there's an investment class, there's
a financial modeling class by the Mars or

00:46:31.114 --> 00:46:35.202
I think what he was trying to do and
really heavily there and

00:46:35.202 --> 00:46:40.350
then maybe even like I did startup garage,
my very last quarter.

00:46:40.350 --> 00:46:42.648
I didn't go to like to maybe
do it a bit earlier and

00:46:42.648 --> 00:46:44.880
then also maybe try the school class.

00:46:44.880 --> 00:46:46.370
I think it'd be really cool.

00:46:46.370 --> 00:46:49.027
&gt;&gt; Can you tell us what startup garages?

00:46:49.027 --> 00:46:53.518
&gt;&gt; Yes, startup garage is a class.

00:46:53.518 --> 00:46:58.414
There are a couple of different
quarters to sort of build

00:46:58.414 --> 00:47:03.415
on from each quarter where you
sort of come with an idea and

00:47:03.415 --> 00:47:07.600
really try to build
a startup up from that idea.

00:47:07.600 --> 00:47:14.830
And you've got a couple mentors
guides who act as professors.

00:47:14.830 --> 00:47:19.354
Most of them are from industry, and
they sort of help guiding through

00:47:19.354 --> 00:47:23.080
that process of helping to
hone in on what is your idea?

00:47:23.080 --> 00:47:26.320
What is the MVP,
the minimum viable product.

00:47:26.320 --> 00:47:26.960
Can you build that?

00:47:26.960 --> 00:47:27.670
Can you test that?

00:47:27.670 --> 00:47:29.290
Can you see there's a market for it?

00:47:30.520 --> 00:47:33.275
And it'll just take you
through that process.

00:47:33.275 --> 00:47:38.137
And it's been some really, they've
been some great startups that actually

00:47:38.137 --> 00:47:42.640
come out there is successful startups
that have come out of that class.

00:47:45.340 --> 00:47:45.840
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:47:46.910 --> 00:47:52.204
&gt;&gt; And as we think back now on to
like the advice and best practice and

00:47:52.204 --> 00:47:58.690
text part where [LAUGH] we get to impart
some of your wisdom from having lived it.

00:47:58.690 --> 00:48:01.852
For folks, maybe we'll tailor
this specifically for folks,

00:48:01.852 --> 00:48:05.732
maybe either coming from your own
professional or educational background or

00:48:05.732 --> 00:48:06.880
from stem in general.

00:48:06.880 --> 00:48:09.652
Do you have any tips, best practices,

00:48:09.652 --> 00:48:15.290
words of wisdom that you could share with
folks as we close out today's events?

00:48:16.350 --> 00:48:21.497
So Graham, maybe we can start with you.

00:48:21.497 --> 00:48:26.412
So something I've said before I
keep on harping back to that is

00:48:26.412 --> 00:48:30.580
just knowing what you want
to get out of the program.

00:48:30.580 --> 00:48:33.442
And I think it is
particularly important for

00:48:33.442 --> 00:48:36.234
STEM people that understand background.

00:48:36.234 --> 00:48:40.976
And knowing because because that isn't
necessarily to say you want to get out,

00:48:40.976 --> 00:48:43.384
finance knowledge or business acumen,

00:48:43.384 --> 00:48:47.017
maybe you have an idea you want
to take three startup garage.

00:48:47.017 --> 00:48:52.817
But I think it's knowing what you want to
get out and then really honing in on that.

00:48:52.817 --> 00:48:56.204
The second comes to
a point someone raised and

00:48:56.204 --> 00:49:00.049
asked about fitting in is
don't worry about that.

00:49:00.049 --> 00:49:03.769
Figure out your story for yourself.

00:49:03.769 --> 00:49:05.727
Be vocal about that.

00:49:05.727 --> 00:49:12.459
And you will fit in by being yourself and
you will find the right place.

00:49:12.459 --> 00:49:15.772
And there was a third thing I was
going to say which I've now forgotten.

00:49:15.772 --> 00:49:22.469
But those two are, I think the third is,
is related to that.

00:49:22.469 --> 00:49:27.572
If you want to focus on academics or
something for whatever you want to focus

00:49:27.572 --> 00:49:32.920
on whatever is important to you,do that
regardless of what other people say.

00:49:32.920 --> 00:49:37.220
And if you say you really want to
learn accounting and people saying,

00:49:37.220 --> 00:49:39.310
why accounting it's so boring.

00:49:39.310 --> 00:49:43.731
If that's what you want to do, that's
what's important to you, ignore them and

00:49:43.731 --> 00:49:44.447
go and do it.

00:49:44.447 --> 00:49:48.612
And remember that everyone is struggling
to figure out what they want to do

00:49:48.612 --> 00:49:49.155
as well.

00:49:49.155 --> 00:49:51.443
You're not alone in that.

00:49:51.443 --> 00:49:54.210
&gt;&gt; What's the saying for
successful speech writing?

00:49:54.210 --> 00:49:55.690
Tell them what you're going to say.

00:49:55.690 --> 00:49:58.020
Then tell them and
then tell them what you just said.

00:49:58.020 --> 00:50:03.163
So, already still practicing some of
those effective communication techniques.

00:50:03.163 --> 00:50:06.527
And then Carl, what about yourself,
any tips, best practices,

00:50:06.527 --> 00:50:08.826
words of wisdom you have for
folks out there?

00:50:08.826 --> 00:50:12.643
&gt;&gt; Yeah Graham kind of stole mine
actually I was going to say, like,

00:50:12.643 --> 00:50:16.100
come in with a list of the things
you want to accomplish and

00:50:16.100 --> 00:50:19.362
try to make sure that your
time is aligned with those.

00:50:19.362 --> 00:50:22.898
So, I came in with three specific
goals I wanted to learn how to

00:50:22.898 --> 00:50:26.774
manage a healthcare organization,
learn better communication,

00:50:26.774 --> 00:50:29.698
interpersonal skills and
learning new language.

00:50:29.698 --> 00:50:34.517
And when looking at my classes when
looking at how I spent my time the clubs I

00:50:34.517 --> 00:50:35.157
joined.

00:50:35.157 --> 00:50:38.504
I was very intentional about doing that.

00:50:38.504 --> 00:50:43.010
That being said, I think there's also
something to be said about exploring and

00:50:43.010 --> 00:50:46.051
trying things that take you
out of your comfort zone.

00:50:46.051 --> 00:50:50.542
So, yes, do come in with goals and
make sure you align your time with them.

00:50:50.542 --> 00:50:54.595
But leave a little bit of space for
a little bit of whimsy and

00:50:54.595 --> 00:50:57.507
last minute decisions and exploration.

00:50:57.507 --> 00:50:58.115
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:50:58.115 --> 00:51:03.081
We have one more poll if you guys if
everyone would be willing to help

00:51:03.081 --> 00:51:04.064
us out here.

00:51:04.064 --> 00:51:08.225
Thank you so much to our panelists,
Carl, Graham for

00:51:08.225 --> 00:51:12.859
sharing your time, your story and
your wisdom with us all.

00:51:12.859 --> 00:51:16.999
I encourage you to go ahead and
if you're interested in learning more,

00:51:16.999 --> 00:51:19.621
including what other events we're having,

00:51:19.621 --> 00:51:23.701
you can create your own personalized
dashboard at MyStanford.mba.

00:51:23.701 --> 00:51:27.703
Where we'll help curate some of the things
that might be of most interest to you with

00:51:27.703 --> 00:51:30.200
an answer of a few short questions.

00:51:30.200 --> 00:51:34.055
So once again, thank you so
much for joining us today and

00:51:34.055 --> 00:51:37.922
I hope to see you again at
some other events this summer.

00:51:37.922 --> 00:51:42.074
Have a wonderful evening, day, morning,
wherever in the world you are.

00:51:42.074 --> 00:51:44.121
Take care and stay safe.

00:51:44.121 --> 00:51:48.989
